  "plan": {
    "goal": "Design a blood-brain barrier perfusion channel reproducing 1.0 Pa shear on human brain microvascular endothelial cells (HCMEC/D3) with mean residence time ≥ 6 s for a drug perfusion study.",
    "rationale": "Target shear of 1.0 Pa is at the upper end of the microvascular endothelial physiological range (0.1-1 Pa) and is a defensible BBB shear target. In a 400 µm × 100 µm channel, 1.0 Pa requires 40 µL/min (viscosity 1e-3 Pa·s, water-like medium). At 40 µL/min the channel volume (4 µL) gives a mean residence time of exactly 6.0 s, meeting the ≥6 s requirement. Reynolds ≈ 2.7 (laminar), pressure drop ≈ 2000 Pa (~20 mbar) is well within syringe-pump range. HCMEC/D3 is a human BBB line (BSL-1); note it forms only a moderately restrictive barrier (TEER 100-240 Ω·cm², ~10× below physiological BBB ~1800-2000 Ω·cm²), so barrier readouts should be interpreted accordingly. Cells seeded at 15,000 cells/cm² over the 0.04 cm² channel floor (~600 cells) to form a monolayer. O2 supply at 40 µL/min vastly exceeds demand (ratio ~7400), no hypoxia risk.",
    "chip": {
